  DROPPED      36              -11.001               -11.617            +0.731
     gap decomposition: observed piece -0.428 dex, predicted piece -0.865 dex (gap = observed - predicted = +0.437)
     Mann-Whitney on log10 g_obs, kept against dropped: p = 0.002
  [PASS] V3 (THE LOAD-BEARING HEADLINE IS MAJORITY-ARITHMETIC, AND UNLIKE THE SLOPE IT IS NOT FLAGGED) the 0.617 dex gap between the objects inside SPARC's coverage and those below it decomposes exactly into an OBSERVED piece and a PREDICTED piece.  The predicted piece -- what the kernel says these objects should do, a function of the very x_i that DEFINES the split -- is twice the observed piece.  A1c flags this identity for the slope and then quotes the binned form of the same identity as 'a factor 6.4 in the same currency with support type held fixed' with no such flag.  Most of that factor is the cut restated   (observed piece -0.428 dex, predicted piece -0.865 dex; predicted/observed = 2.0.  Also: '+0.731 against +0.114 is a factor 6.4' is a ratio of two LOGARITHMS and is not a factor in any physical currency -- in acceleration the gap is 10^0.617 = 4.1)
  [FAIL] V3b (MY OWN ATTACK, PARTIALLY FAILING) I tried to show the kept and dropped groups are indistinguishable in OBSERVED dynamical acceleration, which would have made the headline gap entirely definitional.  They are not indistinguishable: the dropped objects really do sit lower in g_obs.  So the gap is about one third real and two thirds arithmetic, not pure arithmetic   (Mann-Whitney on log10 g_obs, kept against dropped: p = 0.0022 (kept -10.573, dropped -11.001).  ATTACK FAILED at this strength; the weaker V3 statement is what stands)

==========================================================================================================================
V4.  AT FIXED DEPTH THE LOCAL GROUP SPLITS WIDE OPEN -- using the audited file's OWN globular clusters.
==========================================================================================================================
  g05's own Part 4e tabulates four outer-halo globular clusters.  The two that carry this check -- Pal 4 and
  Pal 14 -- have DIRECTLY MEASURED dispersions (Frank et al. 2012; Jordi et al. 2009), not N-body-fitted ones, so
  this comparison does not rest on a model-dependent input.  Pal 14 sits at x_i = 0.0127, between Tucana
  (0.0113) and Antlia B (0.0138).  Both are pressure-supported.  If the residual were a function of depth, as
  'depth and residual are the same axis' requires, they would agree.  Recomputed here from the same numbers:
  object                 class            x_i  residual dex
  Lacerta I              dSph         0.00812        +0.236
  Tucana                 dSph         0.01134        +0.117
  Phoenix                dSph         0.01195        +0.383
  Pal 14                 GC           0.01269        -1.306
  Antlia B               dSph         0.01375        +0.231
  Andromeda VI           dSph         0.01479        +0.422
  Andromeda VII          dSph         0.01561        +0.111
  Leo II                 dSph         0.02059        +0.289
  Pal 3                  GC           0.02665        -0.844
  Sculptor               dSph         0.02915        +0.217
  Fornax                 dSph         0.03371        -0.087
  Pal 4                  GC           0.06107        -0.874
  UGC 4879               dSph         0.06683        -0.091
  Leo I                  dSph         0.07279        -0.021
  [PASS] V4 (THE CLAIM 'NO LOCAL GROUP COMPARISON CAN SEPARATE THEM' IS CONTRADICTED BY THE AUDITED FILE'S OWN SECTION 4e) inside one narrow depth band, 0.008 <= x_i <= 0.10, the Local Group contains pressure-supported objects whose residuals differ by well over a dex, with the globular clusters far BELOW the kernel and the dwarf spheroidals above it.  Depth is therefore NOT the axis the residual runs along, and the Local Group does contain a comparison that separates depth from whatever is driving the residual -- it is in the same file, as check S5, where it is correctly read as dark-matter content.  The confound is with DARK-MATTER FRACTION, not with depth   (in the band: 11 dwarf spheroidals median +0.217 dex, 3 globular clusters median -0.874 dex, separation +1.092 dex at FIXED depth; full spread 1.73 dex.  Pal 14 (x_i = 0.0127) is -1.306 while Tucana (x_i = 0.0113) is +0.117 -- 1.4 dex apart at the same depth)

