V5.  IS DEPTH THE PRIVILEGED AXIS, OR ONE LABEL ON A BUNDLE?  Rank every candidate driver.
==========================================================================================================================
  candidate driver                    Spearman rho          p
  log10 x_i (DEPTH)                         -0.760   4.49e-16
  M_V (luminosity)                          +0.809   1.99e-19
  log10 M_star                              -0.808   2.34e-19
  log10 r_half                              -0.609   2.59e-09
  log10 sigma_los                           -0.389   3.90e-04
  sigma error / sigma (QUALITY)             +0.393   3.45e-04
  log10 x_e (environment)                   +0.579   2.25e-08
  [PASS] V5 (DEPTH IS NOT SINGLED OUT BY THE DATA) ranking every candidate driver of the residual by rank correlation, depth does NOT come first.  Luminosity and stellar mass both track the residual more tightly than depth does, and even a pure DATA-QUALITY proxy -- the fractional error on the measured dispersion, which no theory says should correlate with anything -- reaches p ~ 1e-4.  In this sample faint, small, low-dispersion, poorly measured, close-in and deep are the same objects.  Naming DEPTH as THE confound is a choice among several equally good labels; the defensible statement is that everything is confounded with everything, which is weaker and different from the claim under audit   (ranked by |rho|: M_V (luminosity) +0.81; log10 M_star -0.81; log10 x_i (DEPTH) -0.76; log10 r_half -0.61.  Depth ranks 3 of 7, behind M_V (luminosity) (+0.81).  The data-quality proxy alone reaches rho = +0.39, p = 3.5e-04)
     dynamical-to-baryonic mass ratio inside r_1/2 spans 0.7-1017; Spearman(log M_dyn/M_bar, residual) = +0.945, p = 3.8e-39
     and that ratio itself runs with luminosity: Spearman(M_V, log M_dyn/M_bar) = +0.824, p = 1.2e-20
  [PASS] V5b (WOULD ORDINARY COLD DARK MATTER PRODUCE THE SAME SIGNATURE?  YES, EXACTLY) the residual against this framework's kernel is a monotone relabelling of the dynamical-to-baryonic mass ratio inside the half-light radius, and that ratio rising as dwarfs get fainter is the oldest and least controversial fact about Local Group dwarfs under LCDM.  So the entire pattern the audited file calls a depth trend is reproduced with no modified gravity at all.  It therefore does not discriminate, whichever way it is read   (Spearman(log M_dyn/M_bar, residual) = +0.945 (p = 3.8e-39) on N = 79; Spearman(M_V, log M_dyn/M_bar) = +0.824 (p = 1.2e-20).  The residual axis IS the dark-matter-fraction axis, and the globular clusters in V4 -- zero dark matter -- sit at the opposite end of it)

==========================================================================================================================
V6.  IS 'BELOW SPARC'S COVERAGE' A FACT ABOUT THE DATA OR ABOUT THE WINDOW SETTING?
==========================================================================================================================
  quoted coverage floor (deepest KEPT object) x_i = 0.0113
  SPARC points below it: 15 of 3140, from 10 of 147 galaxies; deepest SPARC point x = 0.00223
  dropped pressure objects that nevertheless sit ABOVE SPARC's deepest measured point: 21 of 36
  [FAIL] V6 (A SECONDARY ERROR IN A1b'S WORDING) A1b states that 'every large pressure-supported residual sits at an internal acceleration BELOW anything SPARC measures'.  That is not so: SPARC's deepest measured point is x = 0.0022, and a majority of the dropped objects sit ABOVE it.  They are dropped because SPARC's points there are too SPARSE to form a 20-point median, not because the data end.  The coverage floor at 0.011 is a density threshold for most of the dropped sample and an absolute one only for the rest -- which is a weaker statement than the file makes, though it does not change the operational conclusion   (21 of 36 dropped objects lie ABOVE SPARC's deepest measured point x = 0.00223; only 15 of 3140 SPARC points, from 10 of 147 galaxies, lie below the quoted floor x = 0.0113, so the sparsity itself is real and the file's operational choice to drop them is defensible)

