==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT ON THE CLAIM
==========================================================================================================================
  CLAIM: 'Support type and acceleration depth are completely confounded in the Local Group, so no Local Group
  comparison can separate them.  This is why the separation dies, and it applies to f09's version of the test
  as much as to this one.'

  WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the operationally important part.
   * The arithmetic reproduces exactly (V1): 36 of 50 dropped, +0.731 against +0.114 dex, slope -0.498,
     r = -0.623 on N = 79, 5.4 shuffle-sigma.  No computational error was found anywhere in the audited file.
   * The withdrawal of f09's 1.73 sigma is UNTOUCHED by anything here.  The matched separation really is
     0.9 sigma, really does move with the matching window, and really is smaller than its own prescription
     systematic.  Nothing in this file rescues f09.
   * There IS a real confound.  What is wrong is its name and the evidence offered for it.

  WHAT DOES NOT SURVIVE.
   1. 'NO LOCAL GROUP COMPARISON CAN SEPARATE THEM' IS CONTRADICTED BY THE SAME FILE, 300 LINES LATER.  In the
      band 0.008 <= x_i <= 0.10 the Local Group holds pressure-supported objects spanning 1.73 dex, the
      globular clusters 1.09 dex below the dwarf spheroidals AT FIXED DEPTH; Pal 14 at x_i = 0.0127 is
      1.4 dex from Tucana at x_i = 0.0113.  Depth therefore does not determine the residual, and check S5 is
      itself a Local Group comparison that separates depth from the driver.  The verdict block ignores it.
   2. THE DRIVER IS DARK-MATTER FRACTION, NOT DEPTH (V5, V5b).  Depth ranks 3 of 7 among candidate
      drivers, behind luminosity and stellar mass; a pure data-quality proxy also tracks the residual at
      p ~ 1e-4; and the residual axis is a monotone relabelling of M_dyn/M_bar (rho = +0.95), so ordinary
      cold dark matter reproduces the whole signature.  'Confounded with depth' is one label on a bundle.
   3. THE EVIDENCE QUOTED FOR THE CONFOUND IS MOSTLY ARITHMETIC, AND A1c NAMES THE WRONG IDENTITY (V2, V3).
      The prescription contributes +0.878 to the slope and the data +0.380 +- 0.055; A1c's stated
      identity assumes +0.5 for the first and 0 for the second, and neither holds.  A constant-valued sample
      reproduces the signature at 8.6 shuffle-sigma against the real 5.4 (M-V2).  The kept/dropped
      headline decomposes -0.428 observed against -0.865 predicted, and unlike the slope it is not flagged.
      'A factor 6.4 in the same currency' is a ratio of two logarithms; the acceleration factor is 4.1.
   4. A SECONDARY WORDING ERROR (V6): 'every large pressure-supported residual sits BELOW anything SPARC
      measures' is false for 21 of the 36 dropped objects.

  WHAT MY OWN ATTACKS FAILED TO SHOW, recorded as failing checks so they cannot be dropped.
   * V2b: the depth trend is NOT pure arithmetic.  The observed accelerations do climb with the baryonic ones
     at +0.380 +- 0.055 dex per dex, 6.9 sigma from zero.  A1c's sentence 'they behave as if set by
     something with a common scale' is refuted, but in the direction of MORE data content, not less.
   * V3b: the kept and dropped groups are NOT indistinguishable in observed acceleration (p = 0.002), so the
     headline gap is roughly a third real and two thirds definitional, not purely definitional.

  THE STATEMENT THAT WOULD SURVIVE A REFEREE, replacing the one under audit:
   * 'The Local Group pressure-supported sample cannot test support type, because the objects carrying the
     residual are dark-matter dominated AND faint AND deep AND poorly measured at once, and the residual against
     this kernel is a relabelling of their dark-matter fraction.  Outer-halo globular clusters at the same
     internal accelerations, with no dark matter, sit far below the kernel -- so the Local Group DOES separate
     support type from dark-matter content, and separates it against support type.'
   * Not: 'depth and support type are completely confounded, so no Local Group comparison can separate them.'

RESULT: 12 checks, 3 FAIL -> ["V2b (MY OWN ATTACK, AND IT FAILS -- RECORDED SO IT CANNOT BE QUIETLY DROPPED) I tried to show the depth trend is PURE arithmetic, with no data content.  It is not.  The observed dynamical accelerations do climb with the baryonic ones, at +0.380 +- 0.055 dex per dex.  So the pressure sample is not a set of objects with one common dynamical acceleration; there is real, if shallow, tracking.  The audited file's interpretive sentence -- 'they behave as if set by something with a common scale' -- is the part that is refuted, while its caution about the slope not being independent evidence stands for a different reason than the one it gives", 'V3b (MY OWN ATTACK, PARTIALLY FAILING) I tried to show the kept and dropped groups are indistinguishable in OBSERVED dynamical acceleration, which would have made the headline gap entirely definitional.  They are not indistinguishable: the dropped objects really do sit lower in g_obs.  So the gap is about one third real and two thirds arithmetic, not pure arithmetic', "V6 (A SECONDARY ERROR IN A1b'S WORDING) A1b states that 'every large pressure-supported residual sits at an internal acceleration BELOW anything SPARC measures'.  That is not so: SPARC's deepest measured point is x = 0.0022, and a majority of the dropped objects sit ABOVE it.  They are dropped because SPARC's points there are too SPARSE to form a 20-point median, not because the data end.  The coverage floor at 0.011 is a density threshold for most of the dropped sample and an absolute one only for the rest -- which is a weaker statement than the file makes, though it does not change the operational conclusion"]   rc=1
